186413339 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413339 1077 "selection" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413339 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413328 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413339 1079 #uuid "93e076d5-a5ae-573d-9e84-8303573a1f36" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413340 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413334 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413340 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413340 1077 "field-1" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413340 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413334 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413340 1079 #uuid "fd5de39b-4f47-50b7-a647-9365f8fad97f" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413337 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1069 "q-88693" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-6}^{6}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1072 #uuid "0c87590b-cc07-5050-b79f-9ad013757661" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413341 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413338 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1069 "q-334371" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1072 #uuid "7e16dfe4-483e-56b2-ae34-2f1386783053" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413342 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413339 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1069 "q-115272" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1071 "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1072 #uuid "93763c90-6504-59a6-ad5e-8ed17cc5ac6d" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413343 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413340 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1069 "q-334355" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1072 #uuid "c1148328-1d0d-53fb-826c-7f5db78f1f65" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413344 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\sqrt{4-(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413345 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413346 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413345 1062 #uuid "0277a93a-ee40-523e-aca9-461457efed21" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413345 1063 #edb/ref 17592186275341 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413346 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413349 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413346 1062 #uuid "5efb4e1f-5f25-58cc-8cad-df223dc54867" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413346 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413344 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413347 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413345 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413347 1062 #uuid "8d2569ba-9a65-57c0-8435-59f5608e5eec" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413347 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413343 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413348 1058 #edb/ref 17592186413347 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413348 1062 #uuid "96c22ae1-3ee6-5184-a661-9475acf9100c" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413348 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413341 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413349 1062 #uuid "bb566f75-c1c5-5c01-ad23-23adc24970e3" 13194139534183 17592186045417 true] [17592186413349 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413342 13194139534183 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"activity-dd7f20dd-b98f-586f-b688-7ce1c3a853b4" 17592186413324 "answer-130e6afa-dce0-5b08-819c-ef94704234fd" 17592186413325 "answer-376dd84b-186c-5a4f-8a0a-85f3f4cc251e" 17592186413326 "answer-47aae119-de52-5a6f-b7a1-ed5cfb5a10e2" 17592186413327 "answer-5a0f7606-ed30-5617-8e4d-36a130ebe24b" 17592186413328 "answer-761e54dd-814f-565c-bb2b-669fb57d2836" 17592186413329 "answer-7c9326e8-8412-5f0f-be79-a57ba80e957f" 17592186413330 "answer-8284560a-94dd-597a-81d2-57c5cccc574d" 17592186413331 "answer-a3cfc328-